{-
   ==========================================================================================
   `Test.Golden` - snapshots - Design-Test.md §7.
   ==========================================================================================

       snapshot("after two inserts", show(m))

   The text is compared against a file, and the file is written by the run that first asked for it.
   That is the same trade every golden in `test/` makes, and §7's four rulings are all about the ways
   the current `generate` gets it wrong. Each one is implemented here and each is worth restating,
   because none of them is what a snapshot library usually does:

    1. **An update rewrites only what the run actually reached.** Nothing here walks the corpus and
       rewrites it; a snapshot is written by the assertion that compared it, so `--filter Map
       --update` touches the `Map` snapshots and nothing else. `generate` rewriting all four hundred
       `.expect` files is why AGENTS.md has to say "copy the directory into `mktemp -d` first".
    2. **An update says what it wrote, and separates a reorder from a change.** A rewritten snapshot
       whose lines are the same multiset in a different order is a *reorder*, reported as one and
       counted apart. Most library reorganizations change goldens only that way, and telling the two
       apart is the difference between reviewing four files and reviewing four hundred.
    3. **A snapshot nothing reached in a full run is stale**, reported by name and deleted by
       `--update --prune`. A golden file that no assertion compares against is the empty-fixture
       directory again: something that verifies nothing must not look like something that verifies
       everything.
    4. **Absence is not "mode off".** A missing snapshot is a *create* in a development run and a
       *failure* under `--no-update`, which is what CI passes. The two are told apart by a flag and
       never by a file being empty - `readExpectedRun` treating an empty `.run.expect` as "skip this
       comparison" while still reporting Pass is the failure mode this ruling exists to forbid.

   > **Two deviations from §7, and both are the tree's rather than the design's.**
   >
   > **The directory is a root plus a flat name, not `<directory of at.file>/__snapshots__/`.** A
   > `@caller` location's `file` is the *module* the call is in - `buildCallerSite` fills it from
   > `sourceModule` - and no filesystem path reaches the resolver at all, so "beside the test that
   > wrote it" is not something this can compute. The snapshot lives at
   > `<root>/<module>.<test>.<name>.txt` with the root defaulting to `__snapshots__` under the
   > working directory and settable with `--snapshot-dir`. Honouring §7 literally needs a `path`
   > alongside `file` in the `@caller` vocabulary, which needs `Location` to carry one.
   >
   > **The diff does not go in the report stream.** §7 asks for unified text with the first differing
   > line called out; the stream is line-oriented precisely so that a crash loses one line (§5.2),
   > and a 40 KB diff in it would defeat that. The report line names the first difference, and the
   > text that was actually produced is written to `<snapshot>.actual` beside the snapshot - so the
   > full diff is `diff` away, in whatever tool the reader already has.
-}

{-
   The whole of the runner's snapshot state, and every initializer is a constant.

   Report.yana explains why that is not a preference: a library module's global has to have a
   constant initializer, so a `[String]` of what was reached is not writable here. The reached set is
   therefore one joined `String`, which is the shape `Section.yana` uses for the same reason. Each
   entry is stored surrounded by newlines - `\nA.txt\n\nB.txt\n` - so that a search for `\nA.txt\n`
   cannot match inside `\nBA.txt\n`, which a plain separator would allow.
-}
let &snapshotRoot = "__snapshots__" :: String
let &reachedFiles = "" :: String

let &createdCount = 0 :: Int
let &changedCount = 0 :: Int
let &reorderedCount = 0 :: Int
let &matchedCount = 0 :: Int
let &skippedCount = 0 :: Int

-- 0 is a development run, 1 is `--update` and 2 is `--no-update`. A number rather than two booleans,
-- because the three are exclusive and a pair of flags can say something that is not one of them.
let &updateMode = 0 :: Int
let &pruneStale = False

{-
   What the command line asked for, read once by the runner before any case runs.

   Called from `runMain` rather than by each assertion, so a snapshot written from a helper five
   frames down still lands where the run was told to put it.
-}
pub fn configureSnapshots(options: Options) -> {}:
    if length(options.snapshotDir) > (0 :: Size) then snapshotRoot = copy(options.snapshotDir)

    updateMode = if options.noUpdate then 2 else if options.update then 1 else 0
    pruneStale = options.prune

{-
   One snapshot, in both moods - §4.2.

   `check`-shaped by default: a mismatch is recorded and the test runs on, because a case that writes
   three snapshots and differs in the first should still say what the other two did.
-}
pub fn snapshot(name: String, text: String, @caller ->at: Site) -> {}:
    match snapshotOutcome(name, text, at):
        Nothing -> {}
        Just(->claim) -> record(Failure {site: at, claim: claim, detail: ""})

pub fn requireSnapshot(name: String, text: String, @caller ->at: Site) -> Test:
    match snapshotOutcome(name, text, at):
        Nothing -> return Ok({})
        Just(->claim) -> return failing(Failure {site: at, claim: claim, detail: ""})

{-
   The comparison, and the four things it can decide.

   Read as a table, which is what rulings 1 and 4 come to in code:

   | on disk | development | `--update` | `--no-update` |
   |---|---|---|---|
   | the same | pass | pass | pass |
   | different | **fail** | rewrite, classified | **fail** |
   | missing | create | create | **fail** |

   The middle cell of the first column is the one worth pointing at: a development run *fails* on a
   difference rather than quietly rewriting it. Rewriting is what `--update` is for, and a tool that
   did it by default is one where a wrong answer becomes the expected answer by being run.

   **Public, and it is the framework's own testability that makes it so.** Every assertion in this
   library is a predicate and a `record`, and the two halves are separable everywhere; here the split
   has to be *reachable*, because a test of what a mismatching snapshot does cannot be written in
   terms of `snapshot` - the assertion would record the failure it was checking for, and the case
   would go red for succeeding. `test/lib/Golden.yana` is written against this.
-}
pub fn snapshotOutcome(name: String, text: String, @caller at: Site) -> Maybe(String):
    let file = snapshotFileName(at, name)

    if !snapshotStoreAvailable():
        {-
           A target with no filesystem - §11.2's F4 note, which is the same asymmetry `@test(aborts)`
           has. Counted and said out loud rather than passed silently: ruling 4 is that no assertion
           is ever skipped by something being absent, and a skip that nothing reports is exactly
           that.
        -}
        skippedCount = skippedCount + 1
        reportLine("skip - snapshot {file} needs a filesystem and this target has none\n")
        return Nothing

    markReached(file)

    match readSnapshotFile(snapshotRoot, file):
        Nothing:
            if updateMode == 2:
                return Just("no snapshot {file} and --no-update forbids creating one - run without it to record what this test produces")

            match writeSnapshotFile(snapshotRoot, file, text):
                Just(->why) -> return Just("could not write the snapshot {file}: {why}")

                Nothing:
                    createdCount = createdCount + 1
                    reportLine("snap  new {file}\n")
                    return Nothing

        Just(existing):
            if existing == text:
                matchedCount = matchedCount + 1
                removeSnapshotFile(snapshotRoot, "{file}.actual")
                return Nothing

            if updateMode != 1:
                {-
                   The received text, beside the snapshot rather than in the report - see the note at
                   the top of this file. A failure to write it is not itself a failure: what is being
                   reported is the mismatch, and a read-only directory should not turn one report
                   into two.
                -}
                writeSnapshotFile(snapshotRoot, "{file}.actual", text)
                return Just("snapshot {file} does not match - {firstDifference(existing, text)}, and what this run produced is beside it in {file}.actual")

            match writeSnapshotFile(snapshotRoot, file, text):
                Just(->why) -> return Just("could not rewrite the snapshot {file}: {why}")

                Nothing:
                    -- Ruling 2. The same lines in a different order is the class most library
                    -- reorganizations produce, and it is the one a reviewer can skip.
                    removeSnapshotFile(snapshotRoot, "{file}.actual")

                    if sameLineMultiset(existing, text):
                        reorderedCount = reorderedCount + 1
                        reportLine("snap  reorder {file}\n")
                    else:
                        changedCount = changedCount + 1
                        reportLine("snap  changed {file}\n")

                    return Nothing

{-
   Ruling 3, which can only be asked of a run that reached everything.

   `full` is the runner's answer to "was this the whole corpus, in this process": a filtered run, a
   shard, and a run whose cases went to children have each reached some of the snapshots by
   construction, and calling the rest stale would be reporting the selection rather than the tree.

   A stale snapshot **fails the run**. That is the same judgement ruling 3 makes and the same one the
   empty-corpus guard makes: a golden nothing compares against is indistinguishable, from the
   outside, from one that passes. `--update --prune` is how it stops being one.
-}
pub fn reviewSnapshots(full: Bool) -> Int:
    if !snapshotStoreAvailable() || !full then return 0

    let files = snapshotFileNames(snapshotRoot)
    let &stale = 0 :: Int
    let &i = 0 :: Size

    while i < length(files):
        let file = files[i]
        i = i + 1

        if !wasReached(file):
            if pruneStale && updateMode == 1:
                match removeSnapshotFile(snapshotRoot, file):
                    Nothing -> reportLine("snap  pruned {file}\n")
                    Just(->why) -> reportLine("snap  stale {file} - could not delete it: {why}\n")
            else:
                stale = stale + 1
                reportLine("snap  stale {file} - no test compared against it\n")

    return stale

{-
   What the run did to the snapshots, for the summary line, and empty when it did nothing to them.

   Written as a statement for the reason Test.yana's own tail is: a conditional whose arms are a
   literal and an owned string is refused by the ownership pass.
-}
pub fn snapshotSummary() -> String:
    let &text = "" :: String

    if createdCount > 0 then text ++= ", {createdCount} {plural(createdCount)} created"
    if changedCount > 0 then text ++= ", {changedCount} {plural(changedCount)} changed"
    if reorderedCount > 0 then text ++= ", {reorderedCount} {plural(reorderedCount)} reordered"
    if skippedCount > 0 then text ++= ", {skippedCount} {plural(skippedCount)} skipped"

    return text

-- One word, because "1 snapshots created" is the sort of line a reader stops at to work out whether
-- the tool is confused. Written as a statement rather than as a conditional expression for the
-- reason Test.yana's own tail is.
fn plural(count: Int) -> String:
    if count == 1 then return "snapshot"
    return "snapshots"

{-
   ==========================================================================================
   Names, membership and text
   ==========================================================================================
-}

-- `<module>.<test>.<name>.txt` - §7's name with §7's directory unavailable, per the note above. The
-- module and the test come from the `@caller` location, so two tests of one module writing a
-- snapshot of the same name are two files rather than one race.
fn snapshotFileName(at: Site, name: String) -> String = "{at.file}.{at.function}.{portableName(name)}.txt"

{-
   A snapshot name is prose, and a file name is not.

   Anything that is not a letter, a digit, a dash or an underscore becomes an underscore, so
   `snapshot("after two inserts", ...)` is `after_two_inserts` and a name with a `/` in it cannot
   name a directory. Deliberately not a hash: the file is meant to be found by a person reading the
   test.
-}
fn portableName(name: String) -> String:
    let &out = String.ofCapacity(length(name))
    let &i = 0 :: Size

    while i < length(name):
        let unit = name.unit(i)
        let keep = (unit >= 48 && unit <= 57) || (unit >= 65 && unit <= 90)
                   || (unit >= 97 && unit <= 122) || unit == 45 || unit == 95

        out.pushUnit(if keep then unit else 95)
        i = i + 1

    return out

fn markReached(file: String) -> {}:
    if wasReached(file) then return
    reachedFiles ++= "\n{file}\n"

fn wasReached(file: String) -> Bool = String.contains(reachedFiles, "\n{file}\n")

{-
   Ruling 2's reorder test: the same lines, however they were arranged.

   A sort would be the obvious way and needs an `Ord` over the lines plus an allocation of both; this
   is the same O(n^2) claim-each-line walk `checkPermutes` makes, for the same reason - it needs only
   equality, and a golden with enough lines for that to matter is one whose diff nobody was going to
   read anyway.
-}
fn sameLineMultiset(before: String, after: String) -> Bool:
    let left = linesOf(before)
    let right = linesOf(after)

    if length(left) != length(right) then return False

    let &claimed = [] :: [Bool]
    let &i = 0 :: Size

    while i < length(left):
        push(claimed, False)
        i = i + 1

    let &j = 0 :: Size

    while j < length(right):
        let &found = False
        let &k = 0 :: Size

        while k < length(left):
            if !claimed[k] && left[k] == right[j]:
                claimed[k] = True
                found = True
                k = length(left)
            else:
                k = k + 1

        if !found then return False
        j = j + 1

    return True

fn linesOf(text: String) -> [String]:
    let &lines = [] :: [String]
    let &at = 0 :: Size

    while at < length(text):
        push(lines, takeLine(text, at))

    return lines

-- One line, and `at` moved past the newline that ended it. The newline itself is not part of the
-- line, so a text with a trailing newline has the same lines as one without - which is what makes a
-- golden whose last line lost its terminator compare as a change of that line rather than of two.
fn takeLine(text: String, &at: Size) -> String:
    let &end = at

    while end < length(text) && text.unit(end) != 10:
        end = end + 1

    let line = String.slice(text, at, end)
    at = if end < length(text) then end + 1 else end
    return line

{-
   Where two texts first part company, as one line of report.

   The line number is what a reader needs to open the file at, and the two texts are what tells them
   whether the change was the one they meant. Both are truncated: a snapshot line can be forty
   thousand characters and a report line should not be.
-}
fn firstDifference(expected: String, actual: String) -> String:
    let &e = 0 :: Size
    let &a = 0 :: Size
    let &number = 1 :: Int
    let &answer = "" :: String

    while length(answer) == (0 :: Size):
        let hasExpected = e < length(expected)
        let hasActual = a < length(actual)

        if !hasExpected && !hasActual:
            -- Reachable only when the two differ in a trailing newline, which `takeLine` reads as no
            -- line at all. Worth a sentence of its own rather than a line number nobody can find.
            answer = "they differ only in whether the text ends with a newline"
        else if !hasExpected:
            answer = "line {number}: the snapshot ends and this run had {showText(takeLine(actual, a))}"
        else if !hasActual:
            answer = "line {number}: this run ends and the snapshot had {showText(takeLine(expected, e))}"
        else:
            let one = takeLine(expected, e)
            let two = takeLine(actual, a)
            if one != two then answer = "line {number}: expected {showText(one)}, got {showText(two)}"

        number = number + 1

    return answer

fn showText(line: String) -> String:
    let limit = 60 :: Size
    if length(line) <= limit then return "`{line}`"
    return "`{String.slice(line, 0, limit - 3)}...`"
